Thanks for tracking this down. I reviewed it and found no blocker.

I pushed one simplification: the fixture now uses `#[Map(transform: 'strtoupper')]` instead of a dedicated callable class, which is the style the other fixtures use, so `ReferenceFormatter` is gone. The transform is still observable. Reverting `ObjectMapper.php` alone makes `testDirectionlessTargetTransformIsNotAppliedToTheSameNameCopy` fail with `'sf-1'` against `'SF-1'`.

Note for the merge-up. On 8.1 and 8.2 the same block carries an extra `&& !$mapping->targetClass` line, so both hunks conflict. The resolution is:

```php
if (null === $mapping->if
    && !$mapping->targetClass
    && $propertyName === $mapping->source
    && ($mapping->target ?? $propertyName) === $propertyName
) {
```

I verified this by patching an 8.2 checkout and running its ObjectMapper suite, which stays green. The three shapes in the table above behave there exactly as they do here: a bare `#[Map(transform: T)]` and a target-only `#[Map(target: 'id', transform: T)]` stop matching, while `#[Map(source: 'id', transform: T)]` keeps matching. No other shape changes, including those carrying a condition or a target class.
